Why Is Demand for Automotive System ICs Increasing?

Vehicle electrification is an important factor influencing demand. Electric vehicles require semiconductor solutions for battery management, electric-drive control, charging, power conversion, and the coordination of vehicle subsystems.

The expansion of advanced driver-assistance systems is another major driver. Features such as driver monitoring, automated braking support, and sensor-based driving assistance require integrated circuits capable of processing information from multiple vehicle sensors and supporting real-time control functions.

In-vehicle infotainment and connectivity are also increasing the demand for advanced automotive electronics. Drivers and passengers increasingly expect digital interfaces, connected services, and integrated information systems, creating additional requirements for processing, memory, and control components.

Semiconductor Innovation Supports More Integrated Vehicle Electronics

Advancements in semiconductor manufacturing and system integration are enabling the development of automotive ICs with improved computing capability and greater functional integration.

Application-specific integrated circuits and systems-on-chip can consolidate functions that might otherwise require multiple components. This can help designers address system complexity, physical-space constraints, and power requirements, depending on the application.

Artificial intelligence and machine learning are also influencing automotive semiconductor development. These technologies can support advanced perception, sensor fusion, and selected predictive functions in increasingly automated vehicles.

However, automotive semiconductor development must account for demanding operating conditions and strict qualification requirements. Performance improvements must be balanced with reliability, lifecycle support, and the safety needs of vehicle systems.

Electric Vehicles and ADAS Create New Opportunities

Electric vehicles are an important end-use segment because their electrical architectures rely on power-management devices, control ICs, and other semiconductors. The integration of battery management, charging, motor control, and thermal management creates a broad set of semiconductor requirements.

ADAS and autonomous-driving applications offer another growth opportunity. These systems require high-performance computing, reliable sensor interfaces, and efficient processing of information from cameras, radar, and other sensing technologies.

Automotive infotainment, body electronics, and connected vehicle functions also contribute to demand. As manufacturers introduce new vehicle platforms, semiconductor suppliers have opportunities to provide more integrated solutions tailored to specific automotive architectures.

Supply Chain, Validation, and Safety Challenges

Automotive system IC manufacturers face challenges associated with supply-chain resilience, long qualification cycles, and specialized product requirements. Automotive-grade semiconductors must satisfy stringent quality and reliability expectations, and changes to qualified components can require extensive validation.

Design complexity is another concern. As more functions become interconnected, manufacturers must ensure that semiconductor components work reliably within larger vehicle systems. Functional-safety requirements, including relevant ISO 26262 processes, can increase engineering and validation demands.

Supply-chain disruptions can also affect vehicle production because automakers and Tier-1 suppliers depend on timely access to qualified semiconductor components. These factors encourage manufacturers to develop supplier relationships, improve planning, and evaluate supply continuity.

